      "math_academy_id": "q-346697",
      "sequence_position": 15,
      "problem": "Consider the area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.25.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area.",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14221930/assets/e8cd8966d69260c77685d349c97eceb1545686d347f6cdb827ad470880797bf5.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+3+4)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[0,1],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area.",
